<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Urmia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> is a large salt lake in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, between the provinces of East and West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. In the early 1st millennium BCE the Mannaeans lived on its southern shores, and the region was contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Urartu and raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. Later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and local dynasties, the lake has shrunk dramatically in recent decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-urmia.jpg" alt="Lake Urmia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_urmia_1984.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> is an endorheic salt lake in the Iranian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, near the Armenian border and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. At its fullest extent it was the largest lake in West Asia, with a surface area of about 6,000 square kilometres, a length of 140 kilometres, a width of 70 kilometres and a maximum depth of 20 metres. It is fed by 13 permanent rivers and many small springs, and almost half its inflow comes from the Zarrineh and Simineh rivers. It has no outflow, and water is lost only by evaporation. Its salt content once reached roughly four times that of seawater.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name has been explained in several ways. Richard Nelson Frye proposed an Urartian origin, while T. Burrow linked it to Indo-Iranian words for wave. A more likely derivation is from Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, ur for city and mia for water, after the nearby city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> name was Chichast, meaning glittering, after the mineral particles in the water. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it Spauta, and the traditional Armenian name, Kaputan tsov, means blue sea.</p><p>Yanik Tepe on the east shore has been settled since about 6000 BCE, and a large group of sites south of the lake includes Dalma Tepe, Teppe Hasanlu, Geoy Tepe and Hajji Firuz Tepe. The Se Girdan kurgans on the south shore have been redated to the second half of the 4th millennium BCE. Assyrian records of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> (858 to 824 BCE) are among the early mentions of the lake. They name Parsuwaš, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and Matai, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, in its area. Shahi Island, the largest of its islands, is the burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a>, who were buried in a castle above cliffs on the shore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the last five hundred years the area has been home to Azerbaijanis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. The Assyrians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> speak Northeastern Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> dialects and belong to the East Syriac churches and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. The lake and its roughly 102 former islands are protected as a national park, and it is both a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve and a Ramsar site. A causeway and the Urmia Lake Bridge, completed in 2008, now divide it into northern and southern halves. Through drought, the damming of its rivers and the pumping of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, the lake had shrunk to about a tenth of its former size by late 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Urmia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Ishpuini and grandson Menua began writing in Urartian, a language related to Hurrian, using a cuneiform script adapted from Assyria, and extended royal power to the religious centre of Musasir in the Zagros, to the lands north of Lake Van and towards <b>Lake Urmia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Scythian horsemen from the steppes north of the Caucasus entered the lands south of the Caucasus and around <b>Lake Urmia</b> in the early 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishkuza">Ishkuza</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muqaddam khans ruled a small agricultural region of orchards and vineyards near <b>Lake Urmia</b>, maintaining their own troops and paying tribute to the dominant power of the moment, including Karim Khan of the Zand Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maragheh">Khanate of Maragheh</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mannaeans (Mannai) appear in Assyrian texts from the 840s BCE, inhabiting north-western Iran around the southern shores of <b>Lake Urmia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mannaea">Mannaea</a></li></ul></section>
